{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1086236-aberrant-a-couple-questions-on-powers","id":1086236,"title":"[Aberrant] A couple questions on powers","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","The Trinity Continuum","Trinity Continuum: Aberrant"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-01T09:57:01-07:00","id":1086236,"title":"[Aberrant] A couple questions on powers","content":"I have a couple questions in regards to how certain powers work/are balanced<br /> <br /> Boost: This power seems to get less and less effective the higher your quantum is. It costs the same amount of experience to simply raise the Mega Attribute as it does to raise boost. Admittedly there is the consideration of also raising the mundane attribute, but it just doesn't seem like that good of a trade off. Am I missing something?<br /> <br /> Claws: Seems to be little reason to buy more than a single dot, and that dot is cheaper than and provides a little more than half the benefit of Errata'd Crush. Any ideas on how to fix this? Edit: I also am curious on how to make a power that uses claws with some range, like some sort of whip. Just take a body mod? Can the ranged extra be added to claws? What about the ranged strength? Along those lines, it says that 1 point of the range strength gives a touch range power 1 meter of range. Do additional levels just add another meter?<br /> <br /> Disorient: This one seems like it might be broken, though it is hard to tell without seeing it in play. The defender has very little chance of fully resisting the power, since the attacker can have a higher dice pool that includes Mega dice and the defender is limited to Willpower. Spending a Willpower point to throw it off helps, but it's not like they can't just keep throwing it back on until you are drained of Willpower points. Is this as powerful as it seems when used in play or am I missing something? This kind of goes for any power resisted by Willpower, just seems like your chances of actually defending against it are pretty damn low.<br /> <br /> Elemental Anima/Mastery: Is there a system for upgrading from one to the other? Say I start the game at Quantum 4 with Elemental Anima. During play I move to Quantum 5, and my character gains a greater degree of power over his element,allowing him to now create it. Do I have to buy Master as a separate power? Does it upgrade automatically? Is there a specific cost to upgrade it? I feel like this might have been covered somewhere but I overlooked it.<br /> <br /> Force Field: How exactly does the wall extra work? My assumption is that an attack that hits the wall has its damage reduced by the soak value, then the remaining damage passes through to its target. Is this correct? Does the Force Field ever break due to damage? My assumption is no since there are no health levels, and the base Force Field does not do so.<br /> <br /> Immobilize: Can someone explain how this works? I understand the reducing dex part, but what does the Strength of the effect have to do with anything? There's a whole part about how much strength the immobilize has but it doesn't state what it actually uses that strength for. How exactly do you break out? If it is by dealing damage, doesn't that make it useless against high Mega Strength characters, as just their auto successes are enough to bypass the soak and fill all the health levels? Isn't a mental Immobilize that requires Willpower to break out completely broken due to Willpower being unable to benefit from Mega-Dice?<br /> <br /> Immolate: When you punch someone while using Immolate, is the Immolate damage soaked separately or added to the damage of the punch.<br /> <br /> Quantum Bolt: I understand that the damage of Quantum Bolt is reduced in comparison to Mega-Strength enhanced melee damage because of the Range advantage but...that advantage seems pretty minimal in a system where Enhanced Movement, Teleport, Flight and Hyperspeed exist. Did White Wolf over estimate the value of Range in this system or am I underestimating it? It just seems that with the number of ways to close the gap in Aberrant that Range isn't as important as it is in other games.<br /> <br /> Telekinesis: How could one create a power that represents the ability to move an object in only one direction? For instance, a kinetic blast type power that doesn't do damage, just knocks the target away from the user.<br /> <br /> Thanks in advance for any help you guys can give!<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":13348,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13348-echoxiii","user":"EchoXIII","date":". 05-01-2017, 01:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13348-echoxiii","name":"EchoXIII","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T15:09:55-07:00","id":1086823,"title":"","content":"No, Boost is basically terrible. Right off hand, I believe buying it with extras may be worthwhile, such that you can use it on others, and buying it affecting another two attributes is cheaper than buying both attribute together I think, but by itself it isn't great.<br /> <br /> Claws is mostly better for being cheaper than Crush, and the damage boost from Claws costs less Quantum. For Range I would assume buying the Range Extra would likely be acceptable.<br /> <br /> Disorient would probably be broken if most targets were not going to have big dice pools already. You might also not survive enough turns to drain them of Willpower if they are QB'ing or chucking Toyotas.<br /> <br /> There is not a system in place for this; however I believe it would be reasonable to request reallocation of spent experience points with your Storyteller.<br /> <br /> Your interpretation sounds correct. Force Fields don't break.<br /> <br /> You break out of an Immobilize by pitting your Strength (or other appropriate attribute depending on the special effects of the Immobilize) versus the &quot;Strength&quot; of the power. You roll Strength, the strength of the Immobilize is rolled and if you win, any successes over &quot;soak&quot; are taken from the Immobilize &quot;Health Levels&quot;; remove all Health Levels and you break free.<br /> <br /> I would add the damage from Immolate.<br /> <br /> Balancing factors among the multiple abilities are difficult considering both the breadth of available choices and all powers being in revision for so long. It's hard to say why anything ended up being definitively balanced one way or another.<br /> <br /> I would just make a new Level 1 power that just does knock back, personally.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-02T16:23:00-07:00","id":1086859,"title":"","content":"Thank you very much for the information. That definitely clears things up for me, especially the parts about Immobilize and Force Field. For my games I've considered making a house rule that Boost can push a Mega Attribute to 2 ranks higher than your normal maximum and see how that goes. My main worry would be it being bought at a single rank and used to buff an attribute that is already maxed since the number of successes needed would be pretty low. Might take another look and see about maybe having it do something based on the rank of Boost directly. Hmmm...I'll have to look things over.<br /> <br /> Again, thank you very much for the clarifications!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13348-echoxiii","name":"EchoXIII","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-05T10:10:04-07:00","id":1088355,"title":"","content":"Additional question that occurred to me<br /> <br /> If a character with Immolate later purchases Bodymorph, how does the Immolate from Bodymorph interact with the individual one they purchased? Can both be active at once? If so, do you add the ratings together to get a new rating, or is the character considered to have two separate Immolates active which do damage separately? Can that scenario also be applied to power techniques that mimic Immolate? So could you, in theory, load up on variations of Immolate, have all of them active at once, and be the ultimate &quot;Do not touch me&quot; nova?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13348-echoxiii","name":"EchoXIII","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-07T19:45:42-07:00","id":1089536,"title":"","content":"I would say yes, they can be active at once, but you are only getting the benefit of the strongest of the two effects. So if a &quot;I turn into fire&quot; nova has both 2 dots worth of Immolate in her Body morph and a three dot Immolate, then she only gets the benefit of the three dot power &quot;burning hotter&quot;.<br /> <br /> Now what I might do as a Storyteller is say the character could buy say, higher levels of Immolate at a discount - such that you only have to buy any levels above whatever you effectively have with Bodymorph - but this isn't really in the rules anywhere.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1136-wolfgar","name":"Wolfgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1136_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"646"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T09:03:15-07:00","id":1089820,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>EchoXIII</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/trinity/trinity-continuum-aberrant/1086236-aberrant-a-couple-questions-on-powers#post1086236\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Elemental Anima/Mastery: Is there a system for upgrading from one to the other? Say I start the game at Quantum 4 with Elemental Anima. During play I move to Quantum 5, and my character gains a greater degree of power over his element,allowing him to now create it. Do I have to buy Master as a separate power? Does it upgrade automatically? Is there a specific cost to upgrade it? I feel like this might have been covered somewhere but I overlooked it.</div> </div> </div> </div>I haven't played Aberrant in a long time, so I can't speak to the specific powers, but in all of my games we have a house rule around &quot;upgrading&quot; abilities (when it makes sense to do so): calculate the difference between the current &quot;cost&quot; of your power (in experience points), and the new cost of the upgraded power, and just pay that difference in XP.<br /> <br /> However, in a situation where the effective cost is the same (like your example, where you're going from one level 3 power to another level 3 power), I would probably just let you swap it over for free. I mean, the difference between Elemental Anima and Mastery is essentially only the source of the element, and the built-in requirement of a Quantum floor means that a given Nova's ability to manipulate that element depends less on a powerset, and more on your ability to tap into Quantum.<br /> <br /> But to your specific question, there is no actual in-game rule in *any* of the White Wolf books I have played that provides a way to do this. So you're going to just have to house-rule it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12757-dao-jones","name":"Dao Jones","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-08T11:59:41-07:00","id":1089871,"title":"","content":"Thank you both for your input! It helps a lot!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13348-echoxiii","name":"EchoXIII","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}